{# The compact banner that opens every page except home.

   Home keeps the full-screen hero. Repeating a 100svh photo on seven more pages
   would put the actual content below the fold everywhere and make the site feel
   like seven landing pages rather than one site with sections — so this is a
   short band instead, ~42svh, with the same blur, scale and overlay as the hero
   so the treatment reads as the same family.

   It reuses .hero-bg's mechanics deliberately: blur() feathers an element's own
   edges, which on a full-bleed image shows as a pale halo down the sides, and
   the scale exists to push that feathered edge out of frame. Don't drop the
   scale while keeping the blur.

   The overlay is heavy for the same reason it is on the hero: the photo comes
   from the admin and could be anything, and the white heading has to stay
   readable over all of it. #}
{% set banner_img = page.media[page.header.banner_image|default('')] %}
{# With no photo yet, this is a plain dark band rather than a blurred
   placeholder. The placeholder graphic is a line-art picture frame: blurred and
   stretched across a banner it reads as a broken image rather than as "a photo
   is coming", which is the opposite of what a placeholder is for. Three
   sections are waiting on photos and this is what they show meanwhile. #}
<section class="page-banner{{ banner_img ? '' : ' page-banner--plain' }}">
  {% if banner_img %}
    <img class="banner-bg" src="{{ banner_img.cropZoom(1600, 700).quality(65).url }}"
         alt="" loading="eager" decoding="async">
    <div class="banner-overlay"></div>
  {% endif %}
  <div class="container banner-inner">
    {% if page.header.eyebrow %}<span class="eyebrow">{{ page.header.eyebrow }}</span>{% endif %}
    <h1>{{ page.title }}</h1>
    {% if page.header.intro %}<p class="banner-lead">{{ page.header.intro }}</p>{% endif %}
  </div>
</section>
